f 17592186045417] [17592186562282 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562282 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562282 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186562054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562282 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "61e2bf24-a108-5cef-9f88-dbe1fef86fa5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562318 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186561986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562318 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562318 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562318 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186561986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562318 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9166490f-e949-5c2b-b086-c9c3c85b54de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562321 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186561779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562321 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562321 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562321 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186561779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562321 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "93fafb0c-e45e-5efb-b68e-b2094c3fa0f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562335 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186561942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562335 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562335 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562335 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186561942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562335 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b03df3ec-0172-5e69-a59d-f8dd469d4012" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562385 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562385 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562385 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562385 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562385 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562385 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562385 #edb/ref 1089 #edb/ref 17592186562386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562385 #edb/ref 1090 #uuid "e424c9c6-6ee7-55cc-ad7e-4f961ce5f753" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562386 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186562524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562386 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186562530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562386 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186562533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562386 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186562544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562386 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186562558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562386 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186562591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562386 #edb/ref 1085 "![](images/7a/7a8bc1c792d05f95436cddb344874ba544f3b28d9f11d30a4f7bbd380846d4ce.png)\n\nMatt wants to build a chicken coop on a rectangular piece of land. The diagonal has a length of $x+6,$ one side of the rectangle has a length of $x,$ and the second side has a length of $a(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562386 #edb/ref 1086 #uuid "9c965847-fddb-50a9-8bf6-395ed4b6617e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562386 #edb/ref 1087 #edb/ref 17592186562530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562402 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562402 #edb/ref 1069 "q-167516" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562402 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562402 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/46/4692bd00e1ba3f3db0d09da40ae2d85cf908c304185846c5dd0247ac6a2411c5.png)\n\nUsing the fact that the area of the piece of land is $108$ square feet, it can be shown that the value of $x$ is given by one of the roots of the polynomial\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-972.\n$$\n\nWhich of the above diagrams could be the graph of $y=p(x)?$ Enter \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\" (without quotes).\n\nThe diagram which shows $y=p(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562402 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "152957b4-dc54-5769-bd44-bd7dec40a548" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562402 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze the given polynomial:\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-972\n$$\n\nThe degree ($n=3$) is odd, and the leading coefficient (${a}_{3}=1$) is positive. Therefore, the polynomial has the following end behavior:\n\n* $p(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n\n* $p(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ \n\nOnly the graph $A$ satisfies these conditions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562404 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562404 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562404 #edb/ref 1069 "q-167519" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562404 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562404 #edb/ref 1071 "The equation ${x}^{2}+12x+108=0$ can be written in the form $(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q$, and solve the equation.\n\n$p+q\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,x=-6\\,\\pm \\,\\text{i}$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562404 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "18714db5-7435-5dc3-be40-cc8ecbd5c18b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562404 #edb/ref 1074 "We first move the constant to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+12x+108 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+12x & =-108\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+12x & =-108 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}-{6}^{2} & =-108 \\\\ (x+6)^{2}-36 & =-108 \\\\ (x+6)^{2} & =-72\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=6,q=-72,$ and $p+q=-66.$\n\nFinally, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+6)^{2} & =-72 \\\\ x+6 & =\\pm \\sqrt{-72} \\\\ x+6 & =\\pm \\sqrt{-36\\cdot 2} \\\\ x+6 & =\\pm 6\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i} \\\\ x & =-6\\pm \\text{i}\\,6\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=-6+6\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=-6-6\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562423 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562423 #edb/ref 1069 "q-167520" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562423 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562423 #edb/ref 1071 "The magnitude of the complex numbers found in part $5$ equals{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562423 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3a956a31-5964-5931-a8c8-e6aacda5b4d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562423 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's pick the complex number $z=-6+6\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}.$\n\nFor a complex number $z=a+b\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFor the complex number $z=-6+6\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i},$ we have $a=-6$ and $b=6\\sqrt{2}.$ So, the magnitude is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}|-6+6\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}| & =\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+(6\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{6}^{2}\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562472 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562472 #edb/ref 1069 "q-167515" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562472 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562472 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the expression for the length $a(x)$ in terms of $x.$\n\n$a(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562472 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a92ecba4-785f-5234-a5b9-74e110a01066" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562472 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's consider the right triangle formed by two sides and a diagonal.\n\n![](images/ba/ba2ba6039f294df10db14d592ecc03f301a483745dfab2758a8fdc7df8a2b7d8.png)\n\nAccording to the Pythagorean theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+6)^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{a}^{2} \\\\ {a}^{2} & =(x+6)^{2}-{x}^{2} \\\\ a & =\\sqrt{(x+6)^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\ a & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+12x+36-{x}^{2}} \\\\ a & =\\sqrt{12x+36} \\\\ a & =\\sqrt{4(3x+9)} \\\\ a & =\\sqrt{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{3x+9} \\\\ a & =2\\sqrt{3x+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562501 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562501 #edb/ref 1069 "q-167517" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562501 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562501 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine which of $x=6$, $x=9,$ and $x=10$ is a root of $p(x),$ and use this information to write down a factor of $p(x)$ in the form $(x-r).$\n\n{{field-1}}is a factor of $p(x)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562501 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "def0feb0-2a39-52c6-9068-aa1b2c2978c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562501 #edb/ref 1074 "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we consider the numbers $6$, $9,$ and $10$ as possible roots. Let's check each of these values in turn.\n\n* $x=6$ is not a root of $p(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =(6)^{3}+3(6)^{2}-972 \\\\  & =216+108-972 \\\\  & =-648 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n* $x=9$ is a root of $p(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(9) & =(9)^{3}+3(9)^{2}-972 \\\\  & =729+243-972 \\\\  & =972-972 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n* $x=10$ is not a root of $p(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(10) & =(10)^{3}+3(10)^{2}-972 \\\\  & =1\\,000+300-972 \\\\  & =328 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the factor theorem, $(x-9)$ is a factor of $p(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562513 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562513 #edb/ref 1069 "q-167518" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562513 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562513 #edb/ref 1071 "The polynomial $p(x)$ can be factored as \n\n$$\np(x)=(x-9)\\cdot q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)$ is a quadratic polynomial. Find $q(x).$\n\n$q(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562513 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f871500c-ae9a-570c-8801-41a03ea03c76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562513 #edb/ref 1074 "To factor $p(x),$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-9)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-9)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-9)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $9$ | $1$ | $3$ | $0$ | $-972$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $9$ | $108$ | $972$ |\n|  | $1$ | $12$ | $108$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-972}{x-9}=({x}^{2}+12x+108),\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-972=(x-9)({x}^{2}+12x+108).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}+12x+108$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac={12}^{2}-4(1)(108)=-288<0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}+12x+108$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative. \n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $p(x)$ over the real numbers is given by \n\n$$\n(x-9)({x}^{2}+12x+108).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562524 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186562533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562524 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1b55fecf-97c1-588b-8abb-dad1dd24e478" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562524 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186562404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562530 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186562591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562530 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3be99005-17dc-5a40-acd0-b07dcde789c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562530 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186562472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562533 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3f70b7ff-0c39-5208-8d2c-11f1125a600b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562533 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186562423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562544 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186562524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562544 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5d8fbef6-550a-5de4-b86a-b63e2109cff5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562544 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186562513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562546 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "62dc4bbb-8f96-5d57-9536-27bda4344483" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562546 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186562385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562558 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186562544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562558 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "93f96480-3463-5547-801b-b3e19c4f8877" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562558 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186562501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562591 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186562558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562591 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fa5d68c6-bc7c-5272-b2bf-5bdba68775cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562591 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186562402 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
